<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Neural Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Neural Plasticity</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2090-5904</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1687-5443</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">14640309</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565421</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.2003.69</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Principles of Force Gradation in Skeletal Muscles</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kernell</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"/><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1">Department of Medical Physiology, University of Groningen, PO Box 196, Groningen 9700 AD, The Netherlands</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*D. Kernell: <email>dhkernell@hotmail.com</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2003</year></pub-date><volume>10</volume><issue>1-2</issue><fpage>69</fpage><lpage>76</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2003.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2003</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>A brief survey is given of how motoneuronsand motor units are used for the gradation Ofmuscle force during motor behavior. Basicproperties of motoneurons and muscle fibers,including major kinds of functional specializationalong the axis of &#x02018;fast&#x02019; vs. &#x02018;slow&#x02019;, are reviewed.The principles underlying the rate and recruitmentgradation of force are described, stressingthat the properties of motoneurons and musclefibers are matched to automate importantaspects of the gradation procedure. Recentinvestigations concerning synaptically evokedchanges in the discharge properties of motoneuronsreceive special attention, including&#x02018;plateau&#x02019; currents and, under appropriateconditions, self-sustained &#x02018;plateau&#x02019; discharges.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>